  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on up-weighting outcomes with probabilities less than 20%. The individual considers an outcome with exactly 20% probability (0 Euros in B), which is at the boundary but not strictly below 20%. The reason would only apply if the threshold is interpreted loosely. The individual's emphasis on the value of the zero-loss outcome aligns with the spirit of the reason—giving heightened attention to rare positive outcomes. However, there is no indication that the individual applied a formal weighting rule or adjusted probabilities; instead, they express a preference for having 'a chance' to avoid loss, which is more consistent with a 'hope for the best' heuristic than a structured weighting of low-probability outcomes. While the focus on the low-probability favorable outcome in B is consistent with the informational focus of the reason, the absence of any reference to probability transformation or comparative evaluation based on weighted sums makes it unlikely that the precise rule was used. The justification is more affective ('I preferred B to have a chance') than computational.",
